d to work.\n\nActually the cleaner approach: If |f(x)-f(y)|/|x-y| > ε/δ, then |x-y| < |f(x)-f(y)|·δ/ε. If |f(x)-f(y)| ≥ ε, then... |x-y| < |f(x)-f(y)|·δ/ε. But UC only says |x-y| < δ ⟹ |f(x)-f(y)| < ε. So contrapositive: |f(x)-f(y)| ≥ ε ⟹ |x-y| ≥ δ. Then |f(x)-f(y)|/|x-y| ≤ |f(x)-f(y)|/δ. But we can't bound |f(x)-f(y)| above on (0,1).\n\nWait — the (a)⟹(b) direction is harder than I thought. Let me re-read the informal solution more carefully.\n\nThe informal solution says both directions work via contrapositive. For (a)⟹(b): If ¬(b), then ∃ε₀ > 0 ∀N > 0 ∃x,y: quotient > N and |f(x)-f(y)| ≥ ε₀. For each n, get x_n,y_n with quotient > n and |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀. Since quotient = |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/|x_n-y_n| > n, we get |x_n-y_n| < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/n. But we need |x_n-y_n| → 0. Since x_n,y_n ∈ (0,1), |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| is bounded by... no it's not!\n\nActually wait — the quotient > n means |x_n - y_n| < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/n. We also have |x_n - y_n| < 1 (since both in (0,1)). But that doesn't directly give |x_n - y_n| → 0 unless |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| is bounded.\n\nHmm, but from quotient > n: |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| = quotient · |x_n-y_n| > n·|x_n-y_n|. And |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀. So ε₀  …[trimmed]"},{"seq":10,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:33:32.519768164Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"UniformContinuousOn.*bounded\\|bounded.*UniformContinuous\\|uc_bounded\\|uniformCon"]},{"seq":11,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:33:35.951537798Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"UniformContinuousOn.*bounded\\|uniformContinuous.*image.*bounded\\|Bornology.IsBou"]},{"seq":12,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:33:44.312865735Z","text":"Let me think about this differently. The (a)⟹(b) direction by contrapositive can be formalized without explicitly proving boundedness. Here's the cleaner approach:\n\nActually, I realize I should take a step back. The problem says \"prove the following are equivalent.\" The key trick that makes (a)⟹(b) work: we don't need to find a single N that works. We use N = ε/δ from UC, and the proof goes:\n\nSuppose quotient > ε/δ. Then |x-y| < |f(x)-f(y)| · δ/ε. **Case 1:** If |f(x)-f(y)| < ε, done. **Case 2:** If |f(x)-f(y)| ≥ ε, then |x-y| ≥ δ (contrapositive of UC). So quotient = |f(x)-f(y)|/|x-y| ≤ |f(x)-f(y)|/|x-y|. We know quotient > ε/δ and |x-y| ≥ δ, so |f(x)-f(y)| > ε|x-y|/δ ≥ ε. Still consistent.\n\nHmm wait, I keep going in circles. Let me try to prove it with a **larger** N.\n\nGiven ε > 0. By UC for ε/2: ∃δ > 0: |x-y| < δ ⟹ |f(x)-f(y)| < ε/2. Also, since f extends continuously to [0,1] (UC on (0,1)), f is bounded: |f(x)| ≤ M for x ∈ (0,1), so |f(x)-f(y)| ≤ 2M.\n\nSet N = 2M/δ. Suppose quotient > N. Then |x-y| < |f(x)-f(y)|/N ≤ 2M/N = δ. So |x-y| < δ. By UC: |f(x)-f(y)| < ε/2 < ε. ✓\n\nThis works! But it requires proving f is bounded (M exists). In Lean, the fact that a UC function on a bounded set has bounded range is available. Let me check:"},{"seq":13,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:33:44.332451120Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"Bornology.IsBounded.image\\|IsBounded.*image_of\\|bounded_image\\|map_bounded\\|Born"]},{"seq":14,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:33:47.632866342Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"IsBounded.*image\\|Bornology.map\\|uniformContinuous.*bounded\\|UniformContinuous.*"]},{"seq":15,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:33:52.033913762Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"UniformContinuous.*IsBounded\\|uniformContinuous.*bornology\\|UniformContinuous.*b"]},{"seq":16,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:33:57.848516126Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"totallyBounded.*image\\|TotallyBounded.*image\\|totallyBounded.*uniformContinuous\\"]},{"seq":17,"type":"text","at":"2026-0