,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:11.522057106Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":58,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:16.084279729Z","text":"I'm overcomplicating this. The goal after `rw [lt_div_iff₀ hxy_pos]` is `N * |x - y| < |f x - f y|`. I have `hxy_lt : |x - y| < ε / N` and `hcontra : ε ≤ |f x - f y|` and `hN_pos : 0 < N`. So `N * |x - y| < N * (ε/N) = ε ≤ |f x - f y|`. Just use `nlinarith`:"},{"seq":59,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:16.091077652Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":60,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:22.838965698Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":61,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:26.552347181Z","text":"Just one error now — wrong lemma name. Let me use `mul_div_cancel` or just provide the fact differently:"},{"seq":62,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:26.553052296Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":63,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:34.184587039Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":64,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:37.428648163Z","text":"The `mul_div_assoc` and `mul_comm` as `nlinarith` hints are causing typeclass issues since they're general lemmas. Let me just provide the numeric fact directly:"},{"seq":65,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:37.429175606Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":66,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:44.669963990Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":67,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:49.544237960Z","text":"The (b)⟹(a) direction compiles cleanly! Only the `sorry` in (a)⟹(b) remains. Now let me tackle the hard direction. \n\nFor (a)⟹(b), I need that UC on (0,1) implies f is bounded on (0,1). Let me check if Mathlib has `UniformContinuousOn` → bounded directly, or use totally bounded:"},{"seq":68,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:49.718919387Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"totallyBounded.*Ioo\\|isBounded_Ioo\\|Metric.isBounded.*interval\\|isBounded_Icc\\|i"]},{"seq":69,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:58.258731804Z","text":"`totallyBounded_Ioo` exists. And `TotallyBounded.image` with `UniformContinuous`. But I'm using `UnifContOn01` (my own definition), not Mathlib's `UniformContinuousOn`. Let me think about the cleanest approach.\n\nActually, let me use a completely different strategy for (a)⟹(b) that avoids boundedness entirely. \n\n**Key insight:** The problem says (0,1). A UC function on (0,1) extends to a continuous function on [0,1]. On the compact set [0,1], f is bounded. But formalizing this extension is complex.\n\n**Simpler approach:** Avoid the boundedness issue by proving (a)⟹(b) via contrapositive using the sequential characterization. If ¬(b), for each n we get x_n, y_n with quotient > n and |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀. Then |x_n - y_n| < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/n. Since |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀ and x_n,y_n ∈ (0,1):\n\nActually, the key observation: from quotient > n, we get |x_n - y_n| < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/n. And from quotient > n: |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| > n · |x_n - y_n|. Since |x_n - y_n| ≤ 1 (both in (0,1)) and |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀:\n\n|x_n - y_n| = |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| / quotient < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| / n\n\nWe need this → 0. The problem is we can't bound |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|. But wait — we CAN bound it! Using UC:\n\nSince f is UC on (0,1), and both x_n, y_n ∈ (0,1), we can bound |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| by chaining UC estimates. Partition [x_n, y_n] into segments of length δ (from UC with ε=1 say). Each segment contributes at most 1 to |f|. There are at most 1/δ + 1 ≈ ⌈1/δ⌉ segments. So |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≤ ⌈1/δ⌉.\n\nThis gives |x_n - y_n| < ⌈1/δ⌉ / n → 0. But formalizing the chaining is messy.\n\n**Cleanest formalizable approach:** Use the fact that `UniformContinuousOn f s` with `s` totally bounded implies `Bornology.IsBounded (f '' s)`. Let me find this in Mathlib:"},{"seq":70,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:36:58.456785636Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"UniformContinuousOn.*TotallyBounded\\|TotallyBounded.*UniformContinuousOn\\|Unifor"]},{"seq":71,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:01.792553694Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topology/UniformSpace/Cauchy.lean"]},{"seq":72,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:11.362225132Z","text":"`TotallyBounded.image` needs `UniformContinuous` (global), not `UniformContinuousOn`. Let me think about whether I can work with `UniformContinuous` instead.\n\nActually, since my `UnifContOn01` is a custom definition (ε-δ on (0,1)), I could bridge it to Mathlib's `UniformContinuousOn` and then use Mathlib's machinery. But this adds complexity. \n\nLet me try a much simpler approach: **prove boundedness directly** from the ε-δ definition.\n\nGiven UC with ε=1, get δ > 0 such that |x-y| < δ ⟹ |f(x)-f(y)| < 1. Fix any point a ∈ (0,1), say a = 1/2. For any x ∈ (0,1), connect a to x by a chain of at most ⌈|x-a|/δ⌉ + 1 steps, each of length < δ. Then |f(x) - f(a)| ≤ ⌈1/δ⌉ · 1 = ⌈1/δ⌉. So |f(x)| ≤ |f(1/2)| + ⌈1/δ⌉.\n\nIn Lean, I can formalize this by noting (0,1) has diameter < 1, and constructing the bound. But the chaining proof is technical. Let me use `nlinarith` with appropriate bounds, or just use Mathlib's bornology API.\n\nActually, let me try an entirely different approach. Instead of boundedness, I'll use the **direct proof for (a)⟹(b)** but with a better choice of N:\n\nGiven ε > 0, UC gives δ for ε. Take any point p ∈ (0,1). For x,y ∈ (0,1) with quotient > N:\n|f(x) - f(y)| = quotient · |x - y| > N · |x - y|.\nIf |x - y| < δ, then |f(x)-f(y)| < ε by UC.\nIf |x - y| ≥ δ, then quotient ≤ |f(x)-f(y)|/δ. But quotient > N doesn't give a contradiction.\n\nSo the direct approach fails when |x-y| ≥ δ. The issue is fundamental: when |x-y| is large, the quotient being large doesn't contradict UC.\n\nThe **only** way to make (a)⟹(b) work is to use boundedness. Let me prove the boundedness lemma directly. \n\nHere's my plan:\n1. Prove `unifCont_on01_bounded`: UC on (0,1) ⟹ ∃ M > 0, ∀ x ∈ (0,1), |f x| ≤ M.\n2. Use M in `unifCont_implies_diffQuot`.\n\nFor the boundedness, I'll use: UC gives δ for ε=1. Chain from 1/2 to any x ∈ (0,1). The chain has at most ⌈2/δ⌉ steps (since |x - 1/2| < 1/2 and each step is δ/2). Each step contributes at most 1. So |f(x) - f(1/2)| ≤ ⌈2/δ⌉.\n\nActually, formalizing the …[trimmed]"},{"seq":73,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:11.503796579Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"Metric.uniformContinuousOn_iff\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/ 2>/dev/null | he"]},{"seq":74,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:16.453765954Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B2 -A8 \"theorem uniformContinuousOn_iff \" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topology/Me"]},{"seq":75,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:19.047199939Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/Pseudo/Defs.lean"]},{"seq":76,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:23.222885318Z","text":"So `Metric.uniformContinuousOn_iff` gives exactly my `UnifContOn01` (modulo `dist` vs `|·|`). Now let me check if there's a direct \"UCOn totally bounded → bounded\" lemma:"},{"seq":77,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:23.241586488Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"TotallyBounded.*image\\|image.*TotallyBounded\\|UniformContinuousOn.*totallyBounde"]},{"seq":78,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:26.423727187Z","text":"Let me search more broadly for how UCOn + totally bounded gives bounded image:"},{"seq":79,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:26.431633333Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"isCompact.*uniformContinuous\\|UniformContinuousOn.*isCompact\\|uniformContinuous."]},{"seq":80,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:29.029202625Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"TotallyBounded.*image\\|totallyBounded_image\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topo"]},{"seq":81,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:43.246269844Z","text":"Let me try a different approach entirely. Instead of boundedness, I'll prove (a)⟹(b) by **contrapositive without sequences**, using the ε-N formulation directly.\n\nIf ¬(b): ∃ε₀ > 0, ∀N > 0, ∃x,y ∈ (0,1), x≠y, quotient > N and |f(x)-f(y)| ≥ ε₀.\n\nFrom this, for N = n (n ∈ ℕ), we get x_n, y_n with:\n- quotient > n: n < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| / |x_n - y_n|\n- |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀\n\nSo: |x_n - y_n| < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| / n.\n\nThe problem is showing |x_n - y_n| → 0. But we also know |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀. From n < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/|x_n-y_n| and |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀:\n\nActually, I just realized: |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/|x_n-y_n| > n means |x_n - y_n| < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/n. And |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| ≥ ε₀ doesn't bound it from above.\n\nBUT: using UC with ε₀, we get δ₀ such that |x-y| < δ₀ ⟹ |f(x)-f(y)| < ε₀. So the contrapositive: |f(x)-f(y)| ≥ ε₀ ⟹ |x-y| ≥ δ₀. This gives |x_n - y_n| ≥ δ₀. But then quotient = |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/|x_n-y_n| ≤ |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/δ₀. Combined with quotient > n: |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| > n·δ₀. So |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| grows at least linearly.\n\nAnd |x_n - y_n| < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/n. But |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| > n·δ₀, so |x_n-y_n| > δ₀. And |x_n - y_n| < |f(x_n)-f(y_n)|/n. Since |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| can grow, this doesn't → 0.\n\nWait, but we also have |x_n, y_n ∈ (0,1)|, so |x_n - y_n| < 1. And quotient > n. So |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| = quotient · |x_n - y_n| < quotient · 1. And quotient > n. So |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| > n · δ₀ ≥ δ₀. But also... hmm.\n\nActually, I think there's a fundamental issue. Let me reconsider whether the statement is even true. Take f(x) = 1/(x(1-x)) on (0,1). This blows up at 0 and 1, so it's NOT uniformly continuous. Good.\n\nTake f(x) = sin(1/x) on (0,1). This is bounded but NOT UC (oscillates faster near 0). Take x_n = 1/(nπ), y_n = 1/((n+1/2)π). Then |x_n - y_n| ≈ 1/n² → 0, |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| = |0 - 1| = 1. So quotient ≈ n² → ∞ with |f(x_n)-f(y_n)| = 1 ≥ ε₀. This shows ¬(b) for ε₀ = 1/2 say. And f is not UC. ✓\n\nNow, is the statement correct? If f is UC, is (b) true? I showed above that it requires boundedness.  …[trimmed]"},{"seq":82,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:43.540380280Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"UniformContinuousOn.*image\\|image.*UniformContinuousOn\\|totallyBounded.*functor\\"]},{"seq":83,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:47.584418725Z","text":"No direct lemma. Let me try yet another approach: use `Metric.isBounded_image_of_uniformContinuousOn` or similar:"},{"seq":84,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:47.861301288Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"isBounded.*uniformContinuousOn\\|Bornology.IsBounded.*uniformContinuous\\|isBounde"]},{"seq":85,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:37:48.091433893Z","text":"[auto-continue 2/8]"},{"seq":86,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:05.525150516Z","text":"Let me take a step back and prove boundedness directly with a self-contained argument. I'll use the chaining approach but keep it simple with a key lemma about finite partitions."},{"seq":87,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:05.525557012Z","tools":["write_file"],"previews":["write_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":88,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:10.558232036Z","text":"Now let me focus on filling the `unifContOn01_bounded` sorry. Let me try using the `UniformContinuousOn.restrict` approach to get `UniformContinuous` on the subtype, then use `TotallyBounded.image`:"},{"seq":89,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:10.696208137Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"UniformContinuousOn.*restrict\\|restrict.*uniformContinuous\\|UniformContinuous.*s"]},{"seq":90,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:13.057996736Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"uniformContinuousOn_iff_restrict\\|to_restrict\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/To"]},{"seq":91,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:17.426952037Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"theorem uniformContinuousOn_iff_restrict\\|lemma uniformContinuousOn_iff_restrict"]},{"seq":92,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:19.696439158Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topology/UniformSpace/Basic.lean"]},{"seq":93,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:28.326626282Z","text":"So `UniformContinuousOn f s ↔ UniformContinuous (s.restrict f)`. And `s.restrict f` is `fun x : s => f x`. Now on the subtype `s = I01`, which is totally bounded. Then `(s.restrict f)` is uniformly continuous, so its image (range) is totally bounded. Let me put this together:"},{"seq":94,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:28.327179069Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":95,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:35.511583150Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -40"]},{"seq":96,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:45.434177799Z","text":"Several issues to fix:\n\n1. `unifContOn01_iff` uses `I01` which is `Ioo 0 1`, but `Metric.uniformContinuousOn_iff` uses `dist` not `|x-y|`. The issue is the bridge is wrong — the `dist` vs `|·|` issue. On ℝ, `dist x y = |x - y|` so it should be fine. The error says the types don't match — the issue is `(unifContOn01_iff f)` is an iff but `.mpr hf` where `hf : UnifContOn01 f` — the problem is that `unifContOn01_iff` already uses `I01` but the iff statement is wrong.\n\nLet me check: `unifContOn01_iff` says `UnifContOn01 f ↔ UniformContinuousOn f I01`. The `Metric.uniformContinuousOn_iff` gives `UniformContinuousOn f s ↔ ∀ ε > 0, ∃ δ > 0, ∀ x ∈ s, ∀ y ∈ s, dist x y < δ → dist (f x) (f y) < ε`. My `UnifContOn01` uses `|x-y|` and `|f x - f y|`. On ℝ, `dist x y = |x - y|` and `dist (f x) (f y) = |f x - f y|`. So the iff should hold but the proof needs `simp [Real.dist_eq]` or similar.\n\n2. `TotallyBounded.image` takes `UniformContinuous f` where `f : α → β`, but `I01.restrict f` is `Subtype I01 → ℝ`, and `h_tb : TotallyBounded I01`. The `TotallyBounded.image` gives `TotallyBounded (f '' I01)` not `TotallyBounded (range (I01.restrict f))`. I should use `TotallyBounded.image` with the actual function `f` and `I01`, but that needs `UniformContinuous f` (global).\n\nLet me fix both issues. For the bridge, I'll make the proof explicit. For the totally bounded image, I'll use the range of the restriction:"},{"seq":97,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:45.435000756Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":98,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:50.822526126Z","text":"Wait, that bridge proof is overcomplicated and probably wrong. Let me simplify. The key realization: `Metric.uniformContinuousOn_iff` gives `dist x y < δ → dist (f x) (f y) < ε`, and my `UnifContOn01` gives `|x - y| < δ → |f x - f y| < ε`. Since `dist a b = |a - b|` on ℝ, these are the same. Let me just use `simp [dist_eq]` or the `show` tactic:"},{"seq":99,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:50.823058954Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":100,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:59.827091644Z","text":"Now fix the boundedness lemma — use `TotallyBounded.image` with the restrict function:"},{"seq":101,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:38:59.827631670Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":102,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:39:04.424786Z","text":"Now fix the error at line 130 (in `unifCont_implies_diffQuot`) — the `hM_le` proof:"},{"seq":103,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:39:04.425356251Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":104,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:39:10.969509899Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A01/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -40"]},{"seq":105,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T19:39:24.554912622Z","text":"Several issues. Let me fix them one at a time:\n\n1. `TotallyBounded.image` — type mismatch with the restrict function. The issue is `h_tb : TotallyBounded I01` where `I01 : Set ℝ`, and the image function is `(I01.restrict f) : I01 → ℝ`, so `TotallyBounded.image` would give `TotallyBounded ((I01.restrict f) '' univ)`. But the function type needs to match. Let me try a different approach — use the image directly.\n\n2. `Metric.isBounded_iff` gives a different shape than I expected.\n\n3. `le_max_iff` needs the second argument to be inferred. Use `max_le` or explicit.\n\nLet me simplify the boundedness proof dramatically — use `Metric.isBounded_iff_ball` or `Bornology.IsBounded.subset`:"},{"seq":106,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T19:39:24.555604871Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A01/l